site/README.md was being published at /README.md — and the guard did not catch it

> Ah yes, I did do that, remake main README, and added README to `site/`

The `site/` one is inside `source:`, so Jekyll copied it **verbatim to
`https://www.fcpublicmedia.org/README.md`**. Measured on `main`, not inferred:
built it in a clean worktree and `_site/README.md` was there.

Nothing sensitive in it. But it is a note written for whoever opens the
repository — *"my file structure isn't supposed to impress you yet"* — sitting at
a public URL where it serves nobody, and it is the same class of accident that
published MANIFEST.md, REDIRECTS.md and RESERVE-DESIGN.md before it. A `.md` with
no front matter is copied rather than rendered.

## Kept where it is, and excluded

It should stay in `site/`. GitHub renders a README when you browse into the
directory holding it, and `site/` is the directory a peer will browse into —
which is the whole point of you writing it. So `README.md` is now the one
document entry on the `exclude:` list, and it is a fixed filename rather than a
list that grows.

## The guard had a hole, and this is the better version of it

`test_nothing_internal_is_published.py` checked for `bin/`, `tests/`, `config/`
and files ending `.py`, `.spec.js`, `package-lock.json`. **A stray `.md` sailed
straight through**, which is embarrassing for a check written specifically to
stop internal documents reaching the public.

It now asserts something stronger and much simpler:

> **`_site` contains no `.md` files at all.**

Jekyll renders a page with front matter to `index.html`. A `.md` in the output
therefore *cannot* be a page — it can only be a source file copied verbatim. So
the whole class is one assertion, and it needs no updating when somebody adds a
document. That is the property the old `exclude:` list never had, and the reason
this is a test rather than a longer list.

Verified both directions: it fails on a planted `_site/PROBE.md` and passes on a
clean build.

```
AssertionError: Lists differ: ['PROBE.md'] != []
```

## Note for the voice you are after

You said you want a voice peers can read and copy. Worth knowing where each file
is read from, because it changes what each is for:

- **the root `README.md`** — GitHub's repository landing page. The first thing a
  peer sees. Yours reads like it is aimed there, which is right.
- **`site/README.md`** — GitHub's view of that directory. A second, narrower
  audience: the peer who has decided to copy you and is now looking at how.
- **`docs/`** — where they go after that.

None of those is a URL on the site, and after this PR none of them is.
